window.__GUIDE_CHUNKS__.push([{"id": "03-025", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 25, "title": "Write down your thoughts and feelings for a few days, but don't expect it to cure anything", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. Fifteen to twenty minutes a day for three to four days.", "plain": "Writing down an upsetting event shows, on average, a small positive effect — but two stricter pooled reviews found no clear effect. It costs almost nothing, so it is worth a try. If the problem is severe, see a clinician instead.", "benefit_text": "Three pooled reviews of expressive writing. The first pooled 146 randomized trials and found a positive but small average effect, r = .075. The second pooled 30 randomized trials and found no clear effect on physical or psychological health. The third pooled 16 randomized trials in cancer patients and survivors and found no clear effect on psychological health, physical health, or quality of life (psychological g = 0.04, 95% CI -0.06 to 0.14). The studies' usual protocol: 3 to 4 sessions of 15 to 20 minutes, writing the deepest thoughts and feelings about an upsetting event.", "sources": "Frattaroli J (2006). Experimental disclosure and its moderators: a meta-analysis. Psychological Bulletin. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.132.6.823>; Mogk C, Otte S, Reinhold-Hurley B, Kröner-Herwig B (2006). Health effects of expressive writing on stressful or traumatic experiences - a meta-analysis. Psycho-Social Medicine. <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19742069/>; Zachariae R, O'Toole MS (2015). The effect of expressive writing intervention on psychological and physical health outcomes in cancer patients--a systematic review and meta-analysis. Psycho-Oncology. <https://doi.org/10.1002/pon.3802>", "notes": "Disputed. The supporting side is a pooled review of 146 studies with a positive but small effect; the opposing side is two pooled reviews restricted to randomized trials, both finding no clear effect. The 3-to-4-session, 15-to-20-minute protocol comes from the earliest experimental design, described in the Mogk review's abstract. If writing spirals into worse distress, stop and call 988. For low mood that does not lift, see item 5 in this chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/pon.3802", "https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.132.6.823", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19742069/"]}, {"id": "04-001", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 1, "title": "Turn \"I plan to do it\" into \"at what time, where, doing what when X happens\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Each goal takes 1 to 2 minutes: write one \"if…then…\" sentence. For example: \"Tuesday at 8 a.m., when I sit at the desk, I start with the introduction.\"", "plain": "Rewriting \"I will do this\" as \"Tuesday at 8 a.m., when I sit at the desk, I start with the introduction\" raises the rate at which goals get done, across dozens of independent tests, by a medium-to-large amount. Only the wording of one sentence changes; it costs no money and no resolve.", "benefit_text": "Pooling 94 independent tests, writing a goal first as an \"if…then…\" plan raised goal achievement. In psychology this is called an implementation intention. Effect size d = 0.65, medium to large. It helped in three places: getting started, holding up when interrupted, and walking away from things that cannot work.", "sources": "Gollwitzer, P. M., & Sheeran, P. (2006). Implementation intentions and goal achievement: A meta-analysis of effects and processes. Advances in Experimental Social Psychology, 38, 69–119. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2601(06)38002-1>", "notes": "It only works on things you actually want to do. If you do not want the goal, no number of sentences helps. Also, these studies mostly used students and mostly looked at health habits — carrying them to work settings is extrapolation.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2601(06"]}, {"id": "04-002", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 2, "title": "Write your exit conditions before you start", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Five minutes at the start, to write \"if <metric> isn't met by <date>, I stop.\" The hard part is honouring it on the day, in front of people.", "plain": "Before starting, write one sentence: if some target is not met by some date, stop. Pooling many studies, this kind of pre-written \"if…then…\" really does help people walk away from courses of action that cannot work. What it saves is all the time you would otherwise keep pouring in.", "benefit_text": "Pooling many implementation-intention studies, a pre-set \"if…then…\" helped people disengage from failing courses of action. The time saved is whatever you would otherwise have kept investing.", "sources": "Gollwitzer, P. M., & Sheeran, P. (2006). Implementation intentions and goal achievement: A meta-analysis of effects and processes. Advances in Experimental Social Psychology, 38, 69–119. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2601(06)38002-1>", "notes": "Set the exit conditions at the start, while you are still calm. Set them later and the money already spent will pull the numbers. When judging, count only future investment and future return — see item 3. \"Being willing to quit\" is only one sub-conclusion of the pooled studies; nobody has measured how much time quitting saves.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2601(06"]}, {"id": "04-003", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 3, "title": "Estimate time from how long similar tasks actually took, not from the plan", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A few minutes before estimating, to look up how long the same kind of task actually took last time.", "plain": "Students estimated they would finish a thesis in about thirty-four days; it took fifty-six, and fewer than a third finished on their own estimate. Another group was asked to recall how long the same kind of task took them before, and their on-time rate doubled from about twenty-nine percent to sixty percent.", "benefit_text": "Students predicted 33.9 days to finish a thesis; it took 55.5. Only 29.7% finished on their own estimate. Another group was asked to link the current task to their past experience before estimating. Their on-time completion rate rose from the control group's 29.3% to 60.0%. The optimism that shrinks estimates disappeared in this group.", "sources": "Buehler, R., Griffin, D., & Ross, M. (1994). Exploring the \"planning fallacy\": Why people underestimate their task completion times. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 67(3), 366–381. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366>; Flyvbjerg, B. (2006). From Nobel Prize to project management: Getting risks right. Project Management Journal, 37(3), 5–15. <https://doi.org/10.1177/875697280603700302>; Halkjelsvik, T., & Jørgensen, M. (2012). From origami to software development: A review of studies on judgment-based predictions of performance time. Psychological Bulletin, 138(2), 238–271. <https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025996>", "notes": "Buehler's study used only Canadian university students (Waterloo), a single small study — about 37 people in one study and about 40 per group in another. Estimating from the history of similar projects is called reference-class forecasting; how engineers do it is in Flyvbjerg. A review of many studies found underestimating is more common than overestimating, but by how much varies a lot with the task and the method.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366", "https://doi.org/10.1037/a0025996", "https://doi.org/10.1177/875697280603700302"]}, {"id": "04-004", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 4, "title": "Give tasks without an external deadline a date of your own", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is honouring the date once set — ignoring it on the day is the same as never setting one.", "plain": "With a deadline set by someone else, about eight in ten students finished before it. On their own estimated time, fewer than four in ten did. When the work got done tracked the deadline almost exactly, and barely tracked their own estimate. So tasks without a deadline need one you set yourself, and you have to honour it.", "benefit_text": "With an external deadline, 80.6% of students finished before it. Only 38.7% finished within their own estimated time. When the work got done followed the deadline almost exactly (r = 0.82), and had only a weak link to their own estimate.", "sources": "Buehler, R., Griffin, D., & Ross, M. (1994). Exploring the \"planning fallacy\": Why people underestimate their task completion times. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 67(3), 366–381. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366>", "notes": "Tell the date to someone else and it starts to work like an externally set one. To be clear, the studies verified that externally set deadlines work. Self-set ones — timeboxing, Pomodoro — have never been directly tested; this is extrapolation.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366"]}, {"id": "04-005", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 5, "title": "Outsource chores when the price is below your hourly rate", "money": "high", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "It costs whatever the service costs, plus one session of math to work out your own hourly rate.", "plain": "People who spend money to buy time report higher life satisfaction. The same money spent buying time, rather than buying things, brings more happiness in the moment.", "benefit_text": "A study sampled 6,271 people across four countries: those who spent money to buy time reported higher life satisfaction. The researchers also ran a real-life experiment: the same amount of money spent on buying time rather than on material goods brought higher momentary happiness.", "sources": "Whillans, A. V., Dunn, E. W., Smeets, P., Bekkers, R., & Norton, M. I. (2017). Buying time promotes happiness. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1706541114>", "notes": "First work out your hourly rate: monthly after-tax income divided by monthly actual working hours. Then look at what the chore costs to outsource. If the outsourcing price is below your hourly rate, and the freed time really goes to earning or resting, outsource it. Everyone's rate and prices differ; the answer has to be computed by you. Also, Whillans measured happiness after buying time, not hours saved.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1706541114"]}, {"id": "04-006", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 6, "title": "Set a hard cap on doomscrolling and short video", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A few minutes of action: turn off push notifications, delete the app, or set a daily time limit on it. The hard part is keeping your finger from opening it again.", "plain": "This is the biggest time item in this section. Cutting one hour a day from it equals three hundred and sixty-five hours a year. Online time also includes work, study and keeping in touch — not all of it is waste.", "benefit_text": "Cutting one hour a day equals 365 hours a year. That arithmetic is the whole benefit; no study has measured how much is actually saved by a cap, or where the freed time goes.", "sources": "Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, Gentzkow M (2020). The Welfare Effects of Social Media. American Economic Review, 110(3), 629–676. <https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20190658>; Hunt MG, Marx R, Lipson C, Young J (2018). No More FOMO: Limiting Social Media Decreases Loneliness and Depression. Journal of Social and Clinical Psychology, 37(10), 751–768. <https://doi.org/10.1521/jscp.2018.37.10.751>", "notes": "The two cited trials are about the \"watching how others are doing\" kind of use: deactivating Facebook for four weeks freed 60 minutes a day, and capping three platforms at 10 minutes a day each cut loneliness and depression in students. For those, see item 6 in the \"Don't waste your energy\" chapter. But no study has measured the cap itself.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20190658", "https://doi.org/10.1521/jscp.2018.37.10.751"]}, {"id": "04-007", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 7, "title": "Skip TV and rolling news; batch what you need at fixed times", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is breaking the habit of opening it on impulse, and you may miss some instant updates.", "plain": "In Canada's 2010 time-use survey, people who watched TV averaged almost three hours of it on the diary day. Moving what you actually want to know into fixed, batched viewing saves that whole block.", "benefit_text": "Statistics Canada's 2010 General Social Survey on time use: TV watchers averaged 2 hours 52 minutes of TV on the diary day. Whatever is not watched is saved in full.", "sources": "Statistics Canada. General Social Survey 2010: time spent watching television. <https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/89-647-x/2011001/hl-fs-eng.htm>", "notes": "Only totals are on hand. No study has measured where the saved time goes after people watch less TV and news. \"Watch it in batches\" is the author's experience.", "urls": ["https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/89-647-x/2011001/hl-fs-eng.htm"]}, {"id": "04-008", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 8, "title": "When deciding whether to continue, count only future cost and future return", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is accepting one fact: the time already put in is gone and cannot be recovered.", "plain": "Money and time already invested make people want to keep going. Pooling dozens of datasets, this tendency is stable. So when judging whether to continue, count only what you still have to put in and what you can still get back. Leave what is already spent out of the math.", "benefit_text": "Money, effort and time already invested make people more inclined to continue — the sunk-cost effect. Pooling 98 datasets, the tendency is confirmed stable. Avoiding it stops you from pouring in more time just so the earlier spending \"won't be wasted\".", "sources": "Arkes, H. R., & Blumer, C. (1985). The psychology of sunk cost. Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes, 35, 124–140. <https://doi.org/10.1016/0749-5978(85)90049-4>; Roth, S., Robbert, T., & Straus, L. (2015). On the sunk-cost effect in economic decision-making: A meta-analytic review. Business Research, 8(1), 99–138. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s40685-014-0014-8>", "notes": "The theatre experiment by Arkes and Blumer ran like this. An Ohio university theatre sold season tickets, randomly giving buyers full price (15 dollars), 2 dollars off, or 7 dollars off. In the first half of the season, the full-price group attended 4.11 shows on average, the other two groups 3.32 and 3.29. In the second half the three groups no longer differed. Roth and colleagues found the effect fades with time, and older people are less affected.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/s40685-014-0014-8", "https://doi.org/10.1016/0749-5978(85"]}, {"id": "04-009", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 9, "title": "No agenda, no meeting; stand when you can", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The organizer spends 5 to 10 minutes writing an agenda before the meeting. Standing meetings need the attendees to go along.", "plain": "Meetings with an agenda sent in advance are more likely to be rated useful by the attendees. In an experiment, seated meetings ran about a third longer than standing ones, while the decisions made came out the same.", "benefit_text": "Two surveys, 958 and 292 people. Meetings with an agenda sent in advance were more likely to be rated effective by attendees, a link that holds up statistically. In another experiment, 55 groups met seated and 56 standing; the seated ones ran 34% longer. The quality of decisions did not differ between the two formats.", "sources": "Leach, D. J., Rogelberg, S. G., Warr, P. B., & Burnfield, J. L. (2009). Perceived meeting effectiveness: The role of design characteristics. Journal of Business and Psychology, 24(1), 65–76. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10869-009-9092-6>; Bluedorn, A. C., Turban, D. B., & Love, M. S. (1999). The effects of stand-up and sit-down meeting formats on meeting outcomes. Journal of Applied Psychology, 84 <https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.84.2.277>", "notes": "The standing-meeting experiment used 5-person groups on lab tasks. Do not copy the conclusion to long meetings or large conferences. Leach measured whether attendees felt the meeting worked, not how much was actually produced.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/s10869-009-9092-6", "https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.84.2.277"]}, {"id": "04-010", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 10, "title": "Cut the number of meetings; use async text where possible", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. It needs colleagues and the boss to go along. The hard part is that writing something clearly takes more effort than saying it out loud.", "plain": "The more meetings in a day, the more tired people are and the heavier their workload feels. Cutting one meeting saves exactly that meeting's length, with no discount.", "benefit_text": "Two surveys of employees working 35 hours a week or more, 676 and 304 people. How much time meetings take had no fixed effect on work attitudes and well-being — it depended on two things: how good the meetings were, and how much the job depended on other people. Another study had people keep a week-long diary: the more meetings in a day, the higher the fatigue and the heavier the felt workload. Time saved equals the length of the meeting you cut.", "sources": "Rogelberg, S. G., Leach, D. J., Warr, P. B., & Burnfield, J. L. (2006). \"Not another meeting!\" Are meeting time demands related to employee well-being? Journal of Applied Psychology, 91 <https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.91.1.83>; Luong, A., & Rogelberg, S. G. (2005). Meetings and more meetings: The relationship between meeting load and the daily well-being of employees. Group Dynamics: Theory, Research, and Practice, 9 <https://doi.org/10.1037/1089-2699.9.1.58>", "notes": "Disputed. Rogelberg 2006 found the link between meeting load and well-being is not one-sided: in jobs that heavily depend on other people, more meetings are not a bad thing. Also, no study has directly compared \"switch to async text\" against meeting — that part is the author's inference.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0021-9010.91.1.83", "https://doi.org/10.1037/1089-2699.9.1.58"]}, {"id": "04-011", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 11, "title": "Break big tasks into subtasks before estimating and starting", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Breaking it down takes 5 to 15 minutes.", "plain": "When estimating, people do not automatically unpack a task into its parts. Listing the subtasks first shortens the estimate's shortfall. And once unpacked, each small step is something you can start right away, which also cuts procrastination.", "benefit_text": "When estimating, people do not spontaneously break a multi-part task — writing a literature review, a discussion, a reference list — into pieces. Listing the subtasks first reduces the planning-fallacy shortfall. Each small step then becomes an action you can start immediately, and procrastination drops with it. Among the strongest predictors of procrastination are dislike of the task itself and pushing the task back.", "sources": "Kruger, J., & Evans, M. (2004). If you don't want to be late, enumerate: Unpacking reduces the planning fallacy. Journal of Experimental Social Psychology, 40(5), 586–598. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2003.11.001>; Steel, P. (2007). The nature of procrastination: A meta-analytic and theoretical review of quintessential self-regulatory failure. Psychological Bulletin, 133 <https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.133.1.65>", "notes": "Break it down to the level where each subtask fits in one sitting; splitting too finely wastes time on its own. Also, Kruger and Evans was only verified from the abstract for direction — the exact percentages were not checked against the original text.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2003.11.001", "https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.133.1.65"]}, {"id": "04-012", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 12, "title": "Treat procrastination as avoiding discomfort, not as laziness", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Only the direction of judgment changes: first ask where the task hurts, then fix that.", "plain": "Procrastination has little to do with laziness, and not much to do with personality either. What best predicts whether someone procrastinates is how aversive the task itself is, how capable they feel of it, and how easily they are pulled away by what is in front of them. So what works is making the task less aversive and the first step smaller — not scolding yourself and pushing harder.", "benefit_text": "A meta-analysis of 691 correlations found the strong, stable correlates of procrastination: how aversive the task is, how far the deadline is, self-efficacy (feeling capable), impulsiveness, and conscientiousness with its facets of self-control, distractibility, organization and achievement motivation. Neuroticism, rebelliousness and sensation-seeking had only weak links. The author explains the pattern with temporal motivation theory: a task's present appeal shrinks fast as its payoff moves further away.", "sources": "Steel, P. (2007). The nature of procrastination: A meta-analytic and theoretical review of quintessential self-regulatory failure. Psychological Bulletin, 133 <https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.133.1.65>", "notes": "The benefit is graded \"mid\" because these studies only point in a direction — none measured hours saved. The concrete moves are in item 1 of this chapter (write it as \"at what time, where, doing what when X happens\"), item 11 (break big tasks into subtasks), and item 4 (give tasks without an external deadline a date of your own). These are pooled correlational studies; they cannot show what causes what.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.133.1.65"]}, {"id": "04-013", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 13, "title": "Put what you need within reach and what tempts you far away", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free, a few minutes once. Put the phone in another room, spread the books and the laptop on the desk.", "plain": "Move something a little further away and people take less of it. Moving the same snacks further away clearly cut how much got eaten — a medium-sized effect. Fewer options on display also clearly cut how many people took it. These tricks need no willpower in the moment and take minutes to set up.", "benefit_text": "A Cochrane review of 24 randomized trials. Moving food further away cut how much was eaten: standardized mean difference -0.60 (95% CI -0.84 to -0.36; 12 studies, 1,098 people, low-certainty evidence), a medium effect. Reducing the number of options cut selection of that food more clearly: standardized mean difference -1.13 (95% CI -1.90 to -0.37; 3 studies, 154 people, low-certainty evidence). Regression showed: the further away and the fewer the options, the stronger the effect.", "sources": "Hollands GJ, Carter P, Anwer S, et al. (2019). Altering the availability or proximity of food, alcohol, and tobacco products to change their selection and consumption. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, 9, CD012573. <https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD012573.pub3>", "notes": "The B grade: all 24 included studies were about food — none on alcohol or tobacco; 14 were lab studies, all from high-income countries. So \"move it further and you'll touch it less\" applied to phones and games is extrapolation with no direct trial. Putting the phone out of sight specifically has been directly tested — see item 8 in the \"Don't waste your energy\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD012573.pub3"]}, {"id": "04-014", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 14, "title": "Put money on the outcome, but first ask whether you'd actually stake it", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Deposit-style schemes need you to put up your own money first, forfeited if you fail. In the trial the stake was 150 US dollars.", "plain": "To force yourself to follow through, two designs work: take a bonus on success; put down a deposit, returned with a bonus only on success. Among people willing to join either, the deposit group succeeded by about thirteen percentage points more. But only about one in seven would take the deposit, versus nine in ten for the bonus — so deposits only work for people willing to stake.", "benefit_text": "A US randomized trial of 2,538 people assigned employees and their family or friends to four incentive schemes or usual care. Reward-type (about 800 US dollars on success) had 90.0% uptake; deposit-type (150 US dollars down, plus 650 dollars reward) had 13.7%. Six-month sustained quit rates: 9.4% to 16.0% across the four incentive schemes, 6.0% for usual care. Reward-type 15.7%, deposit-type 10.2%. But among the 13.7% willing to join either, the deposit type's six-month quit rate beat the reward type by 13.2 percentage points (95% CI 3.1 to 22.8).", "sources": "Halpern SD, French B, Small DS, et al. (2015). Randomized trial of four financial-incentive programs for smoking cessation. New England Journal of Medicine, 372(22), 2108–2117. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1414293>", "notes": "The trial measured quitting smoking; carrying it to theses or fitness is extrapolation, so the benefit is graded \"mid\". When choosing a scheme, first check whether you will actually use it. A method most people refuse has no use for those people, however strong its effect. So pick the kind you will accept — a smaller stake beats no stake. On quitting smoking itself, see the \"Don't die slowly\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1414293"]}, {"id": "04-015", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 15, "title": "Count new habits in months, not in \"21 days\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. What it takes is repeating the same action in the same setting every day, for months.", "plain": "\"Twenty-one days to build a habit\" has no backing. In a twelve-week tracking study, an action becoming automatic took eighteen days at the fast end and two hundred and fifty-four at the slow end. Still finding it hard after two to three weeks is normal. Missing one day does not reset the progress.", "benefit_text": "96 volunteers each picked one eating, drinking or activity action, did it daily in a fixed setting (for example \"after breakfast\") for 12 weeks, and rated their own automaticity daily. 82 people's data were usable; the model fit 62. Days to reach 95% of each person's automaticity ceiling ranged from 18 to 254. The study also found that missing one chance to perform it had no substantial effect on habit formation.", "sources": "Lally P, van Jaarsveld CHM, Potts HWW, Wardle J (2010). How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world. European Journal of Social Psychology, 40(6), 998–1009. <https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674>", "notes": "Only this one study, 96 people, all simple actions (like eating a piece of fruit after lunch). Nobody has measured how long complex ones take — running three times a week, writing daily. So still finding it hard after two weeks is not evidence of failure. To make the repetition actually happen, tie the action to a fixed setting — see item 1 of this chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674"]}, {"id": "04-016", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 16, "title": "For procrastination that hurts, use self-help with trial backing before seeing a therapist", "money": "low", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "The price of a self-help book, or nothing if you use free materials. It takes about ten weeks, one section and one exercise per week.", "plain": "Procrastination bad enough to cause suffering is treatable. Ten weeks of cognitive-behavioural self-help materials — with a guide, and fully on your own, clearly beat doing nothing. Guided and unguided showed no difference, so doing it yourself works too.", "benefit_text": "150 people randomized into three groups: guided self-help, unguided self-help, waitlist control. After ten weeks, on the pure procrastination scale, between-group effect sizes were d = 0.70 (95% CI 0.29–1.10) for the guided group and d = 0.50 (0.10–0.90) for the unguided group; on the irrational procrastination scale, 0.81 (0.40–1.22) and 0.69 (0.29–1.09). Clinically meaningful improvement: 31.3% to 40.0% guided, 24.0% to 36.0% unguided. The two groups did not differ on any outcome.", "sources": "Rozental A, Forsell E, Svensson A, Andersson G, Carlbring P (2015). Internet-based cognitive-behavior therapy for procrastination: A randomized controlled trial. Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology, 83(4), 808–824. <https://doi.org/10.1037/ccp0000023>", "notes": "The subjects were Swedish adults using Swedish-language materials, and this book did not verify an equivalent English-language program — hence the \"mid\" benefit grade. The materials' components are the usual set: behavioural activation, behavioural experiments, stimulus control, and teaching about motivation and work methods. The stimulus-control part is item 13 of this chapter (put what tempts you far away). If procrastination comes with clearly low mood or anxiety, start with item 5 in the \"Don't waste your energy\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/ccp0000023"]}, {"id": "04-017", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 17, "title": "Spend one session learning the shortcuts of the software you use daily", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It takes a one-time investment of several hours to tens of hours to learn. The hard part is that the first stretch is slower than the mouse.", "plain": "Most people who have used Word for years still click toolbar icons. In head-to-head experiments, shortcuts beat menus and icons. Learning is a one-time cost; every use after that pays it back. The longer you do the same kind of work, the more it earns back.", "benefit_text": "A study looked at 251 experienced Word users: most rarely used the faster shortcuts and still clicked icon toolbars. Head-to-head experiments confirmed that of menus, icons and shortcuts, shortcuts are fastest. The time saved accumulates with each daily use. People doing the same kind of work long-term gain the most.", "sources": "Lane, D. M., Napier, H. A., Peres, S. C., & Sándor, A. (2005). Hidden costs of graphical user interfaces: Failure to make the transition from menus and icon toolbars to keyboard shortcuts. International Journal of Human-Computer Interaction. <https://doi.org/10.1207/s15327590ijhc1802_1>", "notes": "Only learn the moves you use more than ten times a day — learning everything is another kind of waste. To be clear, the efficiency experiment had only 6 participants, and the abstract gave no seconds saved per action.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1207/s15327590ijhc1802_1"]}, {"id": "04-018", "chapter_num": 4, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your time", "chapter_file": "04-dont-waste-time.md", "number": 18, "title": "Put commute time first when choosing where to live; shorten the one-way commute", "money": "high", "time": "mid", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "High. It may mean higher rent, or a smaller place. Moving itself takes time.", "plain": "The longer the commute, the worse people feel about how life is going. The loss is not made up in pay; not in housing either. Thirty minutes less each way saves about five hours a week.", "benefit_text": "In May 2024, Statistics Canada put the average one-way commute at 26.4 minutes (23.9 minutes in the 2021 census). German data tracking the same people over years: the longer the commute, the steadily lower the self-reported well-being. And the loss was not compensated in income or housing. A review pulling many studies together confirmed: the longer the commute, the less satisfied people are with it, regardless of transport mode. Thirty minutes less each way saves about 5 hours a week.", "sources": "Stutzer, A., & Frey, B. S. (2008). Stress that doesn't pay: The commuting paradox. Scandinavian Journal of Economics, 110(2), 339–366. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9442.2008.00542.x>; Chatterjee, K., et al. (2020). Commuting and wellbeing: A critical overview of the literature with implications for policy and future research. Transport Reviews, 40(1), 5–34. <https://doi.org/10.1080/01441647.2019.1649317>; Statistics Canada (2024). Average one-way commute time, May 2024. <https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/240826/t003a-eng.htm>", "notes": "Disputed. Chatterjee and colleagues, pooling many studies, found no stable link between commute length and overall life satisfaction. Their view: people largely trade the loss off with better jobs or housing. If you can shift your hours off-peak, the same route also runs shorter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1080/01441647.2019.1649317", "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9442.2008.00542.x", "https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/240826/t003a-eng.htm"]}, {"id": "05-001", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 1, "title": "Insure the earner with term life", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Term life is cheap while you are young and healthy — a working-age adult often pays a few hundred dollars a year. One application; review the amount every few years.", "plain": "If you die, the payout replaces your income so your family keeps the house and the bills get paid. Buy term coverage for the working years, not the pricey permanent kind.", "benefit_text": "Term insurance covers death during the term only, so it costs a fraction of permanent policies that bundle investing — the extra premium funds the insurer, not your family. An earner who dies without cover leaves the household with the same bills and one less income.", "sources": "FCAC insurance topic hub (general reference — the specific claims are standard industry practice, not stated on this page). <https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html>", "notes": "Cover the years your family depends on your income — roughly until the mortgage is paid and the kids earn. If nobody depends on your income, you likely need none.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html"]}, {"id": "05-002", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 2, "title": "Buy plain insurance; treat dividends and \"return of premium\" as non-guaranteed", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to choose. Plain cover costs less than the bundled kind — the difference stays in your pocket. The hard part is accepting that the extra features were never a deal.", "plain": "The dividend on a participating policy is not promised — the insurer declares it each year and can pay zero. \"Return of premium\" riders charge you extra for your own money back. Buy plain cover and save the difference yourself.", "benefit_text": "Participating dividends are declared yearly and are not guaranteed; return-of-premium features charge extra premium for money that was yours anyway, and the built-in \"savings\" earns a return the insurer controls with poor liquidity. Plain term insurance separates protection from saving, so each part can be judged on its own.", "sources": "FCAC insurance topic hub (general reference — the specific claims are standard industry practice, not stated on this page). <https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html>", "notes": "Counterpoint: for someone who truly cannot save, a forced-savings policy imposes discipline — that has real value. The advice above is for people who can manage their own savings. Not investment advice.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html"]}, {"id": "05-003", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 3, "title": "Carry enough auto liability insurance", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Raising liability limits above the legal minimum usually costs a modest amount per year. One call to your insurer.", "plain": "Ontario law requires at least $200,000 of third-party liability coverage. A serious crash can produce a judgment many times that size, and the part above your limit comes out of your own savings.", "benefit_text": "FSRA: Ontario drivers must carry a minimum of $200,000 in third-party liability coverage. Judgments above your limit are paid from your own assets, so the legal minimum leaves most households exposed.", "sources": "FSRA. By law you must carry a minimum of $200,000 in Third-Party Liability coverage. <https://www.fsrao.ca/media/7376/download>", "notes": "Rules differ by province — check your own province's minimum and consider an umbrella policy if you have significant assets.", "urls": ["https://www.fsrao.ca/media/7376/download"]}, {"id": "05-004", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 4, "title": "Never borrow to invest", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free — this one saves money by doing nothing.", "plain": "Borrowing to invest turns a price fall into a forced sale. When your collateral no longer covers the loan, the broker sells you out and the loss becomes real.", "benefit_text": "On margin, falling prices trigger a margin call: top up the account or the broker liquidates your positions at the worst moment. Leverage multiplies losses exactly as fast as gains, and interest accrues the whole time.", "sources": "Wealthsimple. Margin call mechanics. <https://www.wealthsimple.com/en-ca/learn/margin-call>", "notes": "Secondary source (a broker's education page), used here only for the mechanics of margin calls and framed broker-neutrally. Shorting and options carry the same forced-exit risk.", "urls": ["https://www.wealthsimple.com/en-ca/learn/margin-call"]}, {"id": "05-005", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 5, "title": "Buy the whole market with a broad index fund", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to switch; many brokerages sell index funds with no commission.", "plain": "Stock-picking funds as a group own nearly the whole market. Their extra fees come straight out of your return. Repeated resampling tests found few funds earned enough to cover their costs.", "benefit_text": "Fama and French (2010): actively managed US equity funds as a group hold close to the market portfolio, so their higher fees translate one-for-one into lower net returns. Bootstrap tests found few funds earned enough to cover their costs.", "sources": "Fama EF, French KR (2010). Luck versus skill in the cross-section of mutual fund returns. Journal of Finance. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.2010.01598.x>", "notes": "Counterpoint: Harvey and Liu (2022) re-examined the bootstrap method and argued the test was too strict, so \"almost no fund has skill\" may be overstated — the fee drag itself is not disputed. <https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.13123> In Canada, buy a broad-market fund and check the fee in the Fund Facts document before you buy.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.2010.01598.x", "https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.13123"]}, {"id": "05-006", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 6, "title": "Diversify — don't bet on one stock, platform, or property", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Diversification is a choice of what to hold, not an extra purchase.", "plain": "One stock, one platform, one property can all sink at once. Spread the money across assets that do not move in lockstep and keep the same expected return with smaller swings.", "benefit_text": "Markowitz (1952): combining assets that do not move together keeps the portfolio's expected return while shrinking its variance. A single holding carries all of its own risk with no compensation for it.", "sources": "Markowitz H (1952). Portfolio selection. Journal of Finance. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1952.tb01525.x>", "notes": "Diversification across accounts, institutions, and currencies counts too — one brokerage failing should not take everything.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1952.tb01525.x"]}, {"id": "05-007", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 7, "title": "Don't trade frequently", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free — and it saves the commissions, spreads, and taxes that trading burns.", "plain": "Traders who moved in and out the most earned 11.4% a year while the market earned 17.9%. Turnover averaged 75%, and trading costs ate almost the whole gap.", "benefit_text": "Barber and Odean (2000): 66,465 US discount-brokerage households, 1991–1996. The most active traders earned 11.4% a year vs 17.9% for the market; average turnover was 75%, and the gap came almost entirely from trading costs, not worse stock picks.", "sources": "Barber BM, Odean T (2000). Trading is hazardous to your wealth. Journal of Finance. <https://doi.org/10.1111/0022-1082.00226>", "notes": "US data from the 1990s; commissions are lower at Canadian discount brokerages now, but bid-ask spreads and taxes still punish churn.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/0022-1082.00226"]}, {"id": "05-008", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 8, "title": "Stay calm in booms and busts", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is sitting still while everyone panics or brags.", "plain": "In one 18-month boom and bust, small active traders lost about 28% of their starting value through trading. In calm stretches the gap was 1% to 3%.", "benefit_text": "An, Lou, and Shi: Shanghai Stock Exchange daily account data, about 40 million accounts, July 2014–December 2015. The smallest 85% of households lost ¥250 billion through active trading vs buy-and-hold — about 28% of their starting value; the largest 0.5% gained a similar amount, about 31%. In calm 18-month windows the gap was only 1% to 3%.", "sources": "An L, Lou D, Shi D. Working paper. <https://personal.lse.ac.uk/loud/AnLouShi.pdf>", "notes": "Published version: Journal of Monetary Economics (2022). <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmoneco.2022.01.001> The benchmark is buy-and-hold in the same period — the lesson is about trading frequency, not about China.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmoneco.2022.01.001", "https://personal.lse.ac.uk/loud/AnLouShi.pdf"]}, {"id": "05-009", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 9, "title": "Pick the lowest-fee fund in its class", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to compare; switching usually costs nothing.", "plain": "Fees compound against you. A fund charging 1 percentage point more a year leaves about 18% less after 20 years and about 26% less after 30 years, all else equal.", "benefit_text": "Pure arithmetic: a 1 percentage point annual fee drag leaves about 18% less after 20 years (0.99^20) and about 26% less after 30 years (0.99^30), assuming identical pre-fee returns. Fama and French (2010) found few active funds earned enough to cover their costs.", "sources": "Fama EF, French KR (2010). Luck versus skill in the cross-section of mutual fund returns. Journal of Finance. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.2010.01598.x>", "notes": "The compounding is simple math, not a study finding. In Canada the fee is the MER shown in the Fund Facts document — compare it before you buy.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.2010.01598.x"]}, {"id": "05-010", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 10, "title": "Name a beneficiary — and keep the name current", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A form at purchase, updated after big life events.", "plain": "Name the people who get the payout, and update the names after divorce, remarriage, and the birth of a child. Without a current named beneficiary, the money goes into the estate, where creditors and probate take their cut first.", "benefit_text": "A named beneficiary generally keeps the payout out of the estate; an unnamed or outdated one sends it through the estate, where creditors and probate costs take their cut first. The FCAC's insurance guide covers how to name a beneficiary.", "sources": "FCAC. Insurance: life insurance, term life insurance, permanent life insurance and how to name a beneficiary. <https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html>", "notes": "Tell your family where the policies are. Review beneficiaries after divorce, remarriage, or the birth of a child — an ex-spouse left as beneficiary is a classic expensive oversight.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html"]}, {"id": "05-011", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 11, "title": "Walk away from \"high-return, guaranteed\" pitches", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is walking away from easy money.", "plain": "No honest investment offers high returns with no risk. The words \"certain high returns\" are the pitch fraudsters use. Walk away.", "benefit_text": "Promises of high, certain returns are the standard lure of investment fraud — legitimate products cannot promise both at once. Anyone pressuring you to decide now is selling, not advising.", "sources": "Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. <https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm>", "notes": "Report fraud to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre at 1-888-495-8501 and to your local police. Provincial securities commissions publish investor alerts — check before you invest.", "urls": ["https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm"]}, {"id": "05-012", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 12, "title": "Sell losers by a preset rule, not by hope", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is admitting the loss.", "plain": "People sell winners and nurse losers. In one study winners were over 50% more likely to be sold than losers, yet the sold winners beat the kept losers by 3.4 percentage points the next year.", "benefit_text": "Odean (1998): 10,000 US brokerage accounts, 1987–1993. Winners were sold at a 0.148 rate vs 0.098 for losers — winners over 50% more likely to be sold on any given day; the stocks sold beat the stocks held by 3.4 percentage points over the next year.", "sources": "Odean T (1998). Are investors reluctant to realize their losses? Journal of Finance. <https://doi.org/10.1111/0022-1082.00072>", "notes": "In Canada, capital losses can offset capital gains — check CRA rules before selling for tax reasons, and never let the tax tail wag the investment dog.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/0022-1082.00072"]}, {"id": "05-013", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 13, "title": "Keep 3–6 months of expenses within same-day reach", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "The cash earns little; that is the price of the insurance. Keep it in a TFSA or savings account you can reach the same day.", "plain": "Keep 3 to 6 months of expenses where you can reach them the same day. A job loss then costs you savings, not a high-interest loan.", "benefit_text": "A 3-to-6-month cushion of living expenses in an account you can reach immediately means a job loss or illness does not force you into high-interest borrowing or a fire sale of investments. A TFSA works: 2026 contributions up to $7,000, withdrawals tax-free any time.", "sources": "CRA. TFSA: $7,000 for 2024 to 2026. <https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/cra-arc/formspubs/pbg/rc243-sch-a/rc243-sch-a-26e.pdf>", "notes": "**UNVERIFIED:** federal deposit insurance is commonly described as covering up to $100,000 per category per member institution — corroborated by multiple secondary sources (bank disclosure pages), but cdic.ca was not in the research primary set; re-check before citing as primary. 2026-10-01.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/cra-arc/formspubs/pbg/rc243-sch-a/rc243-sch-a-26e.pdf"]}, {"id": "05-014", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 14, "title": "Compare before prepaying the mortgage", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to compare. Prepaying itself moves savings into the house.", "plain": "Paying down the mortgage earns you the mortgage rate, without risk. Skip it only if you can reliably earn more after tax elsewhere. Keep your emergency cash first.", "benefit_text": "Prepaying earns your mortgage rate with no market risk; investing instead only wins if your after-tax return reliably beats it. FCAC: prepayment penalties apply when you pay more than your prepayment privileges or break the term early — check the penalty before acting.", "sources": "FCAC. Mortgage terms and amortization: prepayment penalty costs. <https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/mortgages/mortgage-terms-amortization.html>", "notes": "Interest on a principal-residence mortgage is not tax-deductible in Canada, so compare the mortgage rate against after-tax returns. Never prepay with your emergency fund.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/mortgages/mortgage-terms-amortization.html"]}, {"id": "05-015", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 15, "title": "Ask for the generic drug", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to ask. The generic is usually much cheaper.", "plain": "The pharmacy generic has the same active ingredient and works the same way in your body. Ask for it and pay less.", "benefit_text": "Health Canada requires a generic to be bioequivalent to the brand: same medicinal ingredients, same dose, same rate of absorption. Generics are usually much cheaper than the brand.", "sources": "Health Canada. Access to generic drugs. <https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/drug-products/fact-sheets/access-to-generic-drugs.html>", "notes": "Substitution rules are provincial — your pharmacist decides whether the switch is allowed for your prescription.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/drug-products/fact-sheets/access-to-generic-drugs.html"]}, {"id": "05-016", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 16, "title": "Never pay only the credit-card minimum", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to change. Paying in full saves the interest.", "plain": "The minimum payment is not a deal. It is a loan that charges interest from the day you bought, and the grace period dies with it.", "benefit_text": "FCAC: once you stop paying in full, interest accrues from the purchase date on new spending, and minimum payments barely shrink the balance. The grace period only exists while you pay in full.", "sources": "FCAC. Credit card rights and responsibilities. <https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/201/301/liste_hebdomadaire/2010/full_list/w10-50-F-F.html/collections/collection_2010/acfc-fcac/FC5-23-2-2010-eng.pdf?nodisclaimer=1>", "notes": "If you cannot pay in full, pay as much as possible and stop adding new charges until the balance is clear.", "urls": ["https://epe.lac-bac.gc.ca/100/201/301/liste_hebdomadaire/2010/full_list/w10-50-F-F.html/collections/collection_2010/acfc-fcac/FC5-23-2-2010-eng.pdf?nodisclaimer=1"]}, {"id": "05-017", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 17, "title": "Insure only the losses you cannot absorb", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to rethink. Dropping small-risk cover saves the premium.", "plain": "Insurance costs more than it pays back on average — the difference funds the company. Buy it only for the rare loss that would break you.", "benefit_text": "Premiums exceed expected payouts by the insurer's costs and profit, so insurance is a bad deal on average and a good deal only for losses your savings cannot absorb. Small, survivable risks are cheaper self-insured.", "sources": "FCAC. Insurance: why you may need insurance, making a claim, making a complaint. <https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html>", "notes": "The deductible is the dial — raise it on the big policies and keep the savings.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html"]}, {"id": "05-018", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 18, "title": "Don't buy lottery tickets as a plan", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is skipping the weekly ritual.", "plain": "Lottery tickets are priced to lose. The edge is built into the game, not a matter of luck.", "benefit_text": "Every ticket is priced above its expected payout — the gap funds prizes, retailers, and government revenue, so regular play is a steady transfer from your pocket. A ticket as entertainment is a choice; a ticket as a retirement plan is a leak.", "sources": "RCMP. Fraud prevention: foreign lottery and prize schemes among common fraud types. <https://rcmp.ca/en/alberta/news/2026/03/4351658>", "notes": "\"Lottery winner\" pitches asking for fees or personal details are fraud — the RCMP lists foreign lottery and prize schemes among common scams. Report to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre at 1-888-495-8501.", "urls": ["https://rcmp.ca/en/alberta/news/2026/03/4351658"]}, {"id": "05-019", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 19, "title": "Cancel auto-renewals you forgot", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A 10-to-20-minute sweep of app stores and bank statements.", "plain": "Sweep your subscriptions once. The ones you forgot about stop charging you.", "benefit_text": "Unused subscriptions keep charging until you stop them; one 10-to-20-minute sweep of app stores and bank statements catches them. Wireless contracts must be in plain language under the CRTC Wireless Code, with a 15-day cancellation window — read the renewal clause before signing.", "sources": "CRTC. Your rights as a wireless consumer: plain-language contracts, cancellation rights. <http://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/mobile/prepay.htm>", "notes": "Keep auto-renew on the services you actually use when annual billing is cheaper — the enemy is the forgotten subscription, not the renewal itself.", "urls": ["http://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/mobile/prepay.htm"]}, {"id": "05-020", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 20, "title": "File a T1 every year — and a TP-1 in Quebec", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free with CRA-approved software for simple returns. An hour or two once a year.", "plain": "File every year. The 2026 federal tax-free threshold is $16,452, and filing is what triggers your refunds and benefits.", "benefit_text": "CRA: file a T1 every year. The 2026 federal basic personal amount is $16,452 and the lowest federal rate is 14% on the first $58,523 — filing is how you claim refunds and benefits. Quebec residents also file a TP-1 with Revenu Québec.", "sources": "Department of Finance (2026). Report on federal tax expenditures: 2026 federal basic personal amount $16,452, lowest rate 14% to $58,523. <https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/fin/publications/nrtc-cinr/nrtc-cinr-eng.pdf>", "notes": "Quebec files twice: T1 federally, TP-1 provincially. Late filing forfeits benefits long before it forfeits penalties.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/fin/publications/nrtc-cinr/nrtc-cinr-eng.pdf"]}, {"id": "05-021", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 21, "title": "Review your phone and internet plan yearly", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Fifteen minutes against your actual usage.", "plain": "Check your plan once a year against what you actually use. Old plans cost more than current ones for the same service.", "benefit_text": "Carriers keep raising prices on old plans while selling cheaper ones to new customers. The CRTC Wireless Code requires plain-language contracts, caps data overage at $50 a month and roaming at $100 a month, and lets you cancel within 15 days if unhappy — a yearly check of usage against current plans closes the gap.", "sources": "CRTC. Your rights as a wireless consumer. <http://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/mobile/prepay.htm>", "notes": "Bring your usage numbers to the negotiation — retention offers beat advertised ones when you can quote a competitor.", "urls": ["http://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/mobile/prepay.htm"]}, {"id": "05-022", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 22, "title": "Lock down kids' in-app purchases", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Ten minutes in the device settings.", "plain": "A child with your phone and your saved card is a spending machine. Turn on purchase approvals and take your card off their device. Refunds are possible; prevention is easier.", "benefit_text": "Purchases made by a young child without a parent's knowledge can usually be reversed through the platform's refund process; the practical defense is purchase approvals, a purchase password, and removing saved payment methods from the child's device. On shared wireless family plans, only the account holder can consent to extra charges.", "sources": "CRTC. Your rights as a wireless consumer: consent to additional charges on shared plans. <http://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/mobile/prepay.htm>", "notes": "The CRTC consent rule covers wireless charges specifically; app-store purchases are governed by the platform's terms — set the controls before handing over the device.", "urls": ["http://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/mobile/prepay.htm"]}, {"id": "05-023", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 23, "title": "Teach kids the one-line scam rule", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. One conversation, repeated yearly.", "plain": "Teach one sentence: anyone who tells you to touch a parent's phone is a scammer — that includes reading out a code. Add a second: tell me first, I will not be angry.", "benefit_text": "Youth scams all end the same way — the child is told to operate a parent's phone or read out a verification code. One rule covers every variant, and a no-blame reporting promise is what makes kids actually report.", "sources": "Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. <https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm>", "notes": "Report fraud to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre at 1-888-495-8501 and to local police. Kids who report the same day give the money the best chance of being recovered.", "urls": ["https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm"]}, {"id": "05-024", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 24, "title": "Turn off one-click payments for streams and games", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Five minutes in the payment settings.", "plain": "Turn off one-click payments and set a hard limit before the urge arrives. Sent money does not come back.", "benefit_text": "Money sent to streamers and games is effectively unrecoverable; disabling one-click payments and setting spending limits removes the moment of weakness. Treat tips and in-game spending as entertainment with a fixed budget.", "sources": "Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. <https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm>", "notes": "If a streamer or platform pressures you to pay outside the platform, that is a fraud pattern — report it to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre at 1-888-495-8501.", "urls": ["https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm"]}, {"id": "05-025", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 25, "title": "Ignore crossed-out \"regular prices\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is distrusting the big red tag.", "plain": "Your brain judges \"marked down from $999 to $499\" against the $999 first. That anchor may never have been a real price. Know the everyday price of what you buy.", "benefit_text": "A crossed-out \"regular price\" anchors your sense of the discount even when that price was never charged — for example, a $999 tag cut to $499 feels like a steal regardless of the everyday price. First impressions of a number are hard to adjust away from (Tversky and Kahneman 1974).", "sources": "Tversky A, Kahneman D (1974). Judgment under uncertainty: heuristics and biases. Science. <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.185.4157.1124>", "notes": "Only stockpile what keeps, gets used, and is truly cheaper than the usual price — a \"deal\" on something you would not buy is not a saving.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1126/science.185.4157.1124"]}, {"id": "05-026", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 26, "title": "Treat blind boxes and loot boxes as spent money", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is stopping at the budget.", "plain": "Treat every blind box dollar as spent. Set the budget before you start, and stop when it is gone.", "benefit_text": "Randomized purchases are priced so the average box is worth less than it costs — the thrill is the product. A preset budget is the only defense that works.", "sources": "Ontario Consumer Protection Act. <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general>", "notes": "The cited Act is Ontario's general consumer-protection statute, referenced here only as the background consumer-rights framework; online purchases carry return rights that vary by province — check the terms before the return window closes.", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general"]}, {"id": "05-027", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 27, "title": "Buy collectibles for pleasure, never as a savings plan", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to reframe. The hard part is admitting the \"investment\" story.", "plain": "Buy the watch because you love it. The \"it will hold value\" story is how budgets double.", "benefit_text": "Resale prices for watches, sneakers, and toys are set by fickle secondary markets; buying for pleasure is honest, buying as a savings plan is speculation. Gold jewelry carries design and labor markups that resale ignores.", "sources": "Ontario Consumer Protection Act. <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general>", "notes": "The cited Act is Ontario's general consumer-protection statute, referenced here only as the background consumer-rights framework — the investment claim above is practice advice, not a legal finding.", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general"]}, {"id": "05-028", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 28, "title": "Read the ingredient list; bulk bins don't carry one", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Thirty seconds with the package.", "plain": "If you care what is in it, buy the packaged version and read the ingredient list. Bulk bins do not carry one.", "benefit_text": "Health Canada requires prepackaged foods, in general, to be labelled with the information consumers need for informed decisions — including a list of ingredients, priority allergen information, and nutrition information. Bulk bins carry far less information, so \"not listed\" does not mean \"not present.\"", "sources": "Canadian Food Inspection Agency — food labelling requirements checklist: prepackaged foods must carry ingredient lists, allergen declarations, and nutrition information. <http://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-labels/labelling/industry/requirements-checklist>", "notes": "Priority allergens must be declared — if you or your child has an allergy, the packaged label is the reliable source, not the bin.", "urls": ["http://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-labels/labelling/industry/requirements-checklist"]}, {"id": "05-029", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 29, "title": "Keep the evidence when food is unsafe", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Keep the item, the packaging, and the receipt.", "plain": "Keep the item, the packaging, and the receipt. The seller first, then your provincial consumer office.", "benefit_text": "Complaints succeed on evidence: the item, its packaging, and the receipt decide refunds and recalls. Go to the seller first; if that fails, your provincial consumer office is next — in Ontario, Consumer Protection Ontario mediates disputes.", "sources": "Ontario Consumer Protection Act. <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general>", "notes": "Ontario: Consumer Protection Ontario 1-800-889-9768 (mediates, does not enforce). Quebec: the Office de la protection du consommateur enforces. For illness, also tell your local public health unit.", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general"]}, {"id": "05-030", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 30, "title": "Never prepay more than you can afford to lose", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Pay-as-you-go costs a little more per unit and protects everything.", "plain": "Never prepay more than you can afford to lose. Short terms and written refund terms beat a discount that locks your money in a failing business.", "benefit_text": "Big prepayments turn you into an unsecured creditor of the business; pay-as-you-go or short terms keep your exposure small. Get refund terms in writing before money changes hands.", "sources": "Ontario Consumer Protection Act. <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general>", "notes": "Ontario: Consumer Protection Ontario 1-800-889-9768 mediates disputes. Quebec: the Office de la protection du consommateur enforces its consumer protection law.", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general"]}, {"id": "05-031", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 31, "title": "Use the cooling-off window — and a personal 24-hour rule", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is waiting a day.", "plain": "Ontario gives you 10 days to cancel a door-to-door deal. For everything else, wait 24 hours before buying anything big and non-essential.", "benefit_text": "Ontario's Consumer Protection Act s.43(1) lets you cancel a direct (door-to-door) agreement within 10 days of receiving the written copy. Quebec has its own 10-day right for itinerant merchants — confirm with the OPC. For everything else, a personal 24-hour wait on large non-essential purchases kills most impulse buys.", "sources": "Ontario Consumer Protection Act, 2002, s.43(1). <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general>", "notes": "The Quebec 10-day right is stated as a pointer to the OPC, not as a verified citation — confirm the current rule before relying on it.", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general"]}, {"id": "05-032", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 32, "title": "At the bank, check whether it is a deposit or insurance", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Read the cover before you sign.", "plain": "Read the cover before you sign. If it says insurance company, it is not a deposit, and walking away later costs money.", "benefit_text": "A product sold at a bank counter can be insurance, not a deposit — different guarantees, different surrender penalties. Bank deposits carry federal deposit insurance; insurance contracts do not.", "sources": "FCAC. Insurance: why you may need insurance, making a claim, making a complaint. <https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html>", "notes": "Never sign at the counter under time pressure — take the documents home. If the seller cannot explain the surrender penalty in one sentence, walk away.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/insurance.html"]}, {"id": "05-033", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 33, "title": "Read a new insurance policy inside the free-look window", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Read the policy the week it arrives.", "plain": "New insurance policies come with a short window to change your mind. Read the policy inside that window; after it closes, cancelling costs you.", "benefit_text": "Quebec's Insurers Act s.64 lets you cancel an insurance contract bought without a representative within 10 days of receiving the policy, without penalty. Other provinces vary — read the cancellation terms before the window closes.", "sources": "Right of rescission fact sheet (Quebec Insurers Act s.64, 10-day rescission). <https://api.panda7.ca/files/rescission/right_of_rescission.pdf>", "notes": "Secondary source for the Quebec rule — confirm your province's window in the policy itself. Ontario policies commonly include a review window too; the terms are in your contract.", "urls": ["https://api.panda7.ca/files/rescission/right_of_rescission.pdf"]}, {"id": "05-034", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 34, "title": "Ignore \"we'll get your premiums back\" middlemen", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Call the insurer yourself.", "plain": "Anyone offering to get your premiums back for a fee is selling you your own money. Call the insurer yourself.", "benefit_text": "\"Full-refund\" middlemen take a cut of your own money and harvest your ID in the process. The insurer's own process is the only channel that matters.", "sources": "Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. <https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm>", "notes": "Complaint path: the insurer first, then your provincial regulator (FSRA in Ontario, the AMF in Quebec). Report fraud to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre at 1-888-495-8501.", "urls": ["https://antifraudcentre-centreantifraude.ca/index-eng.htm"]}, {"id": "05-035", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 35, "title": "Skip the extended warranty when you can afford the repair", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is saying no at the till.", "plain": "The warranty costs 10% to 50% of the price because it is mostly profit: over half the price you pay is margin. Skip it when you can afford the repair.", "benefit_text": "Chen, Kalra, and Sun (2009): extended warranties are priced at 10% to 50% of the product price with 50–60% margins — about 18 times a normal retail margin; at Best Buy they were 3–4% of revenue and over 50% of profit (US, 2000s).", "sources": "Chen T, Kalra A, Sun B (2009). Why do consumers buy extended service contracts? Journal of Consumer Research. <https://doi.org/10.1086/605298>", "notes": "Exception: if one broken device would cost you your income (a work laptop with no backup), do the math separately.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1086/605298"]}, {"id": "05-036", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 36, "title": "Boiled tap water is not purified water", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C (disputed)", "grade": "C", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free — this one stops you buying bottled water by the case.", "plain": "Canadian tap water is treated and tested. Boiling kills germs. It does not take chemicals out of the water.", "benefit_text": "Municipal tap water in Canada must meet provincial drinking-water standards, and boiling kills microbes. But boiling does not remove dissolved chemicals — including lead from old service lines and plumbing.", "sources": "Health Canada. Water Talk: minimizing exposure to lead from drinking water distribution systems. <https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/environmental-workplace-health/reports-publications/water-quality/water-talk-minimizing-exposure-lead-drinking-water-distribution-systems.html>", "notes": "Counter-evidence: Health Canada's lead guideline (maximum acceptable concentration 0.005 mg/L) exists because old pipes leach lead, and boiling does not remove lead. If your home has old plumbing, use cold water for drinking and cooking, flush the tap after water sits unused, or use a certified filter. Bottled water as a daily habit remains an expensive answer to a problem most taps do not have.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/environmental-workplace-health/reports-publications/water-quality/water-talk-minimizing-exposure-lead-drinking-water-distribution-systems.html"]}, {"id": "05-037", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 37, "title": "Use the RRSP or TFSA — don't leave tax shelter on the table", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to open. The contribution is savings, not spending.", "plain": "RRSP: deduct now, pay tax later. TFSA: pay tax now, never again. The 2026 caps are $33,810 for RRSP and $7,000 for TFSA.", "benefit_text": "CRA 2026: RRSP room is 18% of 2025 earned income up to $33,810; contributions are deductible and withdrawals taxed. TFSA 2026 limit is $7,000: contributions are after-tax, growth and withdrawals are tax-free.", "sources": "CRA. RRSP, DPSP, TFSA limits and the YMPE. <https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/registered-plans-administrators/pspa/mp-rrsp-dpsp-tfsa-limits-ympe.html>; CRA. TFSA: $7,000 for 2024 to 2026. <https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/cra-arc/formspubs/pbg/rc243-sch-a/rc243-sch-a-26e.pdf>", "notes": "If you pay no income tax, RRSP deductions are worth little — check your Notice of Assessment for your room and never exceed it; excess contributions are penalized.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/cra-arc/formspubs/pbg/rc243-sch-a/rc243-sch-a-26e.pdf", "https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/registered-plans-administrators/pspa/mp-rrsp-dpsp-tfsa-limits-ympe.html"]}, {"id": "05-038", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 38, "title": "Online: trust the written terms, keep the screenshots", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Screenshot the listing and the return policy before you pay.", "plain": "Save the listing, the receipt, and the return terms. The sales pitch is not the contract.", "benefit_text": "Screenshots of the listing, the chat log, and the return policy decide disputes — not the pitch. Keep them until the return window closes; check a livestream seller's business identity before buying.", "sources": "Ontario Consumer Protection Act. <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general>", "notes": "Dispute path: the seller, then the platform, then your provincial consumer office — Ontario: Consumer Protection Ontario 1-800-889-9768 (mediates); Quebec: the Office de la protection du consommateur (enforces).", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/02c30/v15?search=general"]}, {"id": "05-039", "chapter_num": 5, "chapter_title": "Don't Waste Money", "chapter_file": "05-dont-waste-money.md", "number": 39, "title": "Pay per gym visit until the habit is proven", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Pay-per-visit costs more per visit — that is the price of the option to quit.", "plain": "Members on monthly plans paid over $17 per visit while a 10-visit pass cost $10 a visit. The monthly crowd overpaid $600 on average.", "benefit_text": "DellaVigna and Malmendier (2006): 7,752 US gym members over 3 years. Monthly-contract members averaged 4.3 visits a month at over $17 a visit vs $10 on a 10-visit pass, overpaying $600 on average; monthly members were also 17% more likely to still be paying after a year without going.", "sources": "DellaVigna S, Malmendier U (2006). Paying not to go to the gym. American Economic Review. <https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.96.3.694>", "notes": "If you have a year of steady twice-a-week attendance behind you, the annual pass wins — buy the commitment only after the habit exists.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.96.3.694"]}, {"id": "06-001", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 1, "title": "Do not cut calcium to prevent kidney stones", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. It is also less hassle — no need to watch every food for calcium.", "plain": "Men told to cut calcium are often told exactly wrong. In a five-year trial, recurrent stone formers who ate normal calcium — with less animal protein and salt — relapsed at half the rate of the low-calcium group. Britain's guideline also says plainly not to restrict calcium.", "benefit_text": "A 5-year randomized trial of 120 men with recurrent calcium-oxalate stones and high urinary calcium. One group ate normal calcium (30 mmol a day) with less animal protein (52 g a day) and less salt (50 mmol sodium chloride a day); the other ate the traditional low-calcium diet (10 mmol a day). Recurrences in 5 years: 12 of 60 versus 23 of 60, relative risk 0.49 (95% CI 0.24–0.98) — the normal-calcium group relapsed at half the rate. The UK's NICE guideline NG118 (1.8.1) says not to restrict daily calcium intake.", "sources": "Borghi L, Schianchi T, Meschi T, et al. (2002). Comparison of two diets for the prevention of recurrent stones in idiopathic hypercalciuria. New England Journal of Medicine, 346(2), 77-84. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa010369>; National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (2019). Renal and ureteric stones: assessment and management. NICE guideline NG118. <https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng118/chapter/Recommendations>", "notes": "Disputed. The trial only covered men with recurrent calcium-oxalate stones and high urinary calcium — not every stone type. The treatment group changed three things at once (normal calcium, less animal protein, less salt), so not all the benefit belongs to \"not cutting calcium\". What is ruled out is actively cutting calcium from food; that is not the same as taking extra. This was about calcium in daily meals; calcium supplements are a separate question — ask your doctor if you have had stones.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa010369", "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng118/chapter/Recommendations"]}]);
